     "result_text_tail": " to FY2023's 24% of sales, with promotional depth kept in stores near ValuMart.\n  - Shrink down to the 2.3% the best-staffed stores already run.\n  - Private label continuing its FY2023\u201325 pace.\n  - The gain from the nine closures split in proportion to store count, because the pack gives only combined figures.\n- **Executive summary:** one sub-point per section instead of two or three, so the page reads at full type size.\n\n**Review status**\n- The issue tree tested nine hypotheses: four held, three held with a different size or condition, and two were wrong. The two wrong ones were management's \"wages are the main cause\" and the e-commerce team's \"e-commerce is profitable\". I left out three options on purpose: overhead cuts, a general price rise and closing delivery.\n- The workbook grades 137 slide numbers B (taken directly from the pack) and 26 E (my estimates, each showing how it was built). None reach A, because without web access nothing could be checked against a second source.\n- Three warnings remain, and I judged each one acceptable. On the delivery-fee slide, the sidebar cites the fee itself, which is not one of the charted results. On the \"what comes first\" slide, the sidebar cites the $4.7M covenant need and the $25M budget, which are not computed from the chart.\n\nTo make the plan firmer, the most useful additions would be markdown dollars by vendor, the lease date and nearest Harlow store for each of the nine stores, and how many staff are paid near the minimum wage."
